\strong{More information about the XG3}

The term XG3 refers to specific groundwater level measurements that can be
calculated for each hydrological year.

Groundwater level dynamics can be described by 'characteristic average
groundwater levels' (also called GXG in Dutch).
These are long-term averages of the (highest,lowest, and spring)
groundwater levels.
The so-called XG3 are used for the calculation of these characteristic
average groundwater levels.

XG3 is a general term for:
\itemize{
\item HG3: the 3 highest groundwater levels measured in a given hydrological
year with at least 14 days between two measurements,
\item LG3: the 3 lowest groundwater levels measured in a given hydrological
year with at least 14 days between two measurements,
\item VG3: 3 groundwater levels measured as closed as possible to April 1,
in the period between March 1 and May 1, with at least 12 days between two
measurements. A VG3 can therefore consist of measurements from both the
current and the previous hydrological year.
}

An XG3 calculation is only performed if the following quality criteria are
met:
\itemize{
\item there are at least 20 measurements per hydrological year,
\item there are no more than 30 days between two measurements.
}

\strong{Which \code{vert_crs} should you use?}

This function can retrieve water levels in m relative to ground level
(\code{"local"}) or in m relative to a standard sea level measured in Ostend.
(\code{"ostend"}). Using m relative to ground level is helpful for ecological
or agricultural applications, for instance: can the root zone come into
contact with groundwater?
The option (\code{"ostend"}) can be used to understand the relationships
between water levels throughout the landscape, for instance: are the water
levels in a stream lower than the groundwater levels?

\strong{When should you use truncated XG3?}

When we measure water levels in a piezometer, we actually do not measure the
groundwater level itself, but rather the hydraulic head at the filter level
(perforated part). The hydraulic head measures how high water will rise in
a well or tube (a piezometer) due to a combination of water pressure and
elevation.
The difference between groundwater level and hydraulic head can be
significant if the filter of the piezometer is deep and/or if there is a lot
of seepage/infiltration (vertical groundwater movement).
Truncated groundwater levels (that is: level measurements that are above
soil surface level are capped to the soil surface level itself) can be used
when you are interested in the actual groundwater levels and if you know that
there is a significant difference between the measured head and this
groundwater level, for instance when the groundwater head in a seepage
area rises much higher than the ground level even though the ground level
isn't actually flooded.
}
